"Strong homotopy types, nerves and collapses (https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.2954)\nciteturn4academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.3 years ago; Title: Strong homotopy types, nerves and collapses\nAuthors: Jonathan Ariel Barmak, Elias Gabriel Minian ... An elementary move in this setting is called a strong collapse and it is a particular kind of simplicial collapse.\nTitle: Strong homotopy types, nerves and collapses\nAuthors: Jonathan Ariel Barmak, Elias Gabriel Minian\nDate: Fri Jul 17 04:52:02 2009\n\nWe introduce the theory of strong homotopy types of simplicial complexes. Similarly to classical simple homotopy theory, the strong homotopy types can be described by elementary moves. An elementary move in this setting is called a strong collapse and it is a particular kind of simplicial collapse. The advantage of using strong collapses is the existence and uniqueness of cores and their relationship with the nerves of the complexes. From this theory we derive new results for studying simplicial collapsibility with a different point of view. We analyze vertex-transitive simplicial $G$-actions and prove a particular case of the Evasiveness conjecture for simplicial complexes. Moreover, we reduce the general conjecture to the class of minimal complexes. We also strengthen a result of V. Welker on the barycentric subdivision of collapsible complexes. We obtain this and other results on collapsibility of polyhedra by means of the characterization of the different notions of collapses in terms of finite topological spaces.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSTRONG HOMOTOPY TYPES, NERVES AND COLLAPSES (https://people.kth.se/~jbarmak/strong.pdf)\nciteturn4search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; STRONG HOMOTOPY TYPES, NERVES AND COLLAPSES\nJONATHAN ARIEL BARMAK AND ELIAS GABRIEL MINIAN ... An elementary strong collapse K ցցe K ∖v consists of removing from\nSTRONG HOMOTOPY TYPES, NERVES AND COLLAPSES\nJONATHAN ARIEL BARMAK AND ELIAS GABRIEL MINIAN\nAbstract. We introduce the theory of strong homotopy types of simplicial complexes.\nSimilarly to classical simple homotopy theory, the strong homotopy types can be de-\nscribed by elementary moves.\nAn elementary move in this setting is called a strong\ncollapse and it is a particular kind of simplicial collapse. The advantage of using strong\ncollapses is the existence and uniqueness of cores and their relationship with the nerves of\nthe complexes. From this theory we derive new results for studying simplicial collapsibil-\nity with a diﬀerent point of view. We analyze vertex-transitive simplicial G-actions and\nprove a particular case of the Evasiveness conjecture for simplicial complexes. Moreover,\nwe reduce the general conjecture to the class of minimal complexes. We also strengthen\na result of V. Welker on the barycentric subdivision of collapsible complexes. We obtain\nthis and other results on collapsibility of polyhedra by means of the characterization of\nthe diﬀerent notions of collapses in terms of ﬁnite topological spaces.\n1. Introduction\nThe notion of simplicial collapse, introduced by J.H.C. Whitehead in the late thirties\n[25], is a fundamental tool in algebraic topology and in combinatorial geometry [5, 9].\nCollapsible polyhedra are the centre of various famous problems, perhaps the most relevant\nof them being the Zeeman Conjecture [26]. Although an elementary simplicial collapse\nis very simple to describe, it is not easy to determine whether a simplicial complex is\ncollapsible. In fact, the Zeeman Conjecture, which states that K × I is collapsible for\nany contractible polyhedron K of dimension 2, is still an open problem. One of the main\ndiﬃculties when studying collapsibility, or simple homotopy theory in general, is the non-\nuniqueness of what we call cores. A core of a complex K, in this setting, is a minimal\nelement in the poset of all subcomplexes which expand to K. For instance, there exist\ncollapsible complexes which collapse to nontrivial subcomplexes with no free faces.\nIn this article we introduce and develope the theory of strong homotopy types of simpli-\ncial complexes, which has interesting applications to problems of collapsibility. Similarly\nto the case of simple homotopy types, the strong homotopy types can be described in terms\nof elementary moves. A vertex v of a simplicial complex K is called dominated if its link\nis a simplicial cone. An elementary strong collapse K ցցe K ∖v consists of removing from\na simplicial complex K the open star of a dominated vertex v. Surprisingly the notion of\nstrong homotopy equivalence also arises from the concept of contiguity classes. It is not\nhard to see that a strong collapse is a particular case of a classical collapse. This new\nkind of collapse is much easier to handle for various reasons: the existence and unique-\nness of cores, the fact that strong homotopy equivalences between minimal complexes are\nisomorphisms and the relationship between this theory and the nerves of the complexes.\n2000 Mathematics Subject Classiﬁcation. 57Q10, 55U05, 55P15, 55P91, 06A06, 05E25.\nKey words and phrases. Simplicial complexes, simple homotopy types, collapses, nerves, ﬁnite spaces,\nposets, non-evasiveness, simplicial actions.\nThe authors’ research is partially supported by Conicet and by grant ANPCyT PICT 17-38280.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStrange Random Topology of the Circle (https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.16270)\nciteturn4academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.5 years ago; Title: Strange Random Topology of the Circle\nTitle: Strange Random Topology of the Circle\nAuthors: Uzu Lim\nDate: Thu May 25 17:26:48 2023\n\nWe characterise high-dimensional topology that arises from a random Cech complex constructed on the circle. Expected Euler characteristic curve is computed, where we observe limiting spikes. The spikes correspond to expected Betti numbers growing arbitrarily large over shrinking intervals of filtration radii. Using the fact that the homotopy type of the random Cech complex is either an odd-dimensional sphere or a bouquet of even-dimensional spheres, we give probabilistic bounds of the homotopy types. By departing from the conventional practice of scaling down filtration radii as the sample size grows large, our findings indicate that the full breadth of filtration radii leads to interesting systematic behaviour that cannot be regarded as \"topological noise\".--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStrong homotopy types, nerves and collapses - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/0907.2954v1)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.3 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; ## Strong homotopy types, nerves and collapses\n\nJonathan Ariel Barmak, Elias Gabriel Minian ... # • BIB • PDF • ARXIV • ... An elementary move in this setting is called a strong collapse and it is a particular kind of simplicial collapse.\n\n## Strong homotopy types, nerves and collapses\n\nJonathan Ariel Barmak, Elias Gabriel Minian\n\nPublished: 2009-07-17\n\nComments: 24 pages, 8 figures\n\nNote:\n\nDOI: 10.1007/s00454-011-9357-5\n\nhomotopy simplicial complex\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe introduce the theory of strong homotopy types of simplicial complexes. Similarly to classical simple homotopy theory, the strong homotopy types can be described by elementary moves. An elementary move in this setting is called a strong collapse and it is a particular kind of simplicial collapse. The advantage of using strong collapses is the existence and uniqueness of cores and their relationship with the nerves of the complexes. From this theory we derive new results for studying simplicial collapsibility with a different point of view. We analyze vertex-transitive simplicial $G$-actions and prove a particular case of the Evasiveness conjecture for simplicial complexes. Moreover, we reduce the general conjecture to the class of minimal complexes. We also strengthen a result of V. Welker on the barycentric subdivision of collapsible complexes. We obtain this and other results on collapsibility of polyhedra by means of the characterization of the different notions of collapses in terms of finite topological spaces.\n\n### BibTeX\n\n[Button: arXiv BibTeX ] [Button: Published BibTeX ]\n\nLoading...\n\n[Button: Copy to Clipboard]\n\nLoading...\n\n[Button: Copy to Clipboard]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSimple Homotopy Types and Finite Spaces (https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0611158)\nciteturn4academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.0 years ago; Title: Simple Homotopy Types and Finite Spaces\nAuthors: Jonathan Ariel Barmak, Elias Gabriel Minian ... We define the concept of collapse of a finite space and prove that this new notion corresponds exactly to the concept of a simplicial collapse.\nTitle: Simple Homotopy Types and Finite Spaces\nAuthors: Jonathan Ariel Barmak, Elias Gabriel Minian\nDate: Mon Nov  6 23:29:33 2006\n\nWe present a new approach to simple homotopy theory of polyhedra using finite topological spaces. We define the concept of collapse of a finite space and prove that this new notion corresponds exactly to the concept of a simplicial collapse. More precisely, we show that a collapse of finite spaces induces a simplicial collapse of their associated simplicial complexes. Moreover, a simplicial collapse induces a collapse of the associated finite spaces. This establishes a one-to-one correspondence between simple homotopy types of finite simplicial complexes and simple equivalence classes of finite spaces. We also prove a similar result for maps: We give a complete characterization of the class of maps between finite spaces which induce simple homotopy equivalences between the associated polyhedra. Furthermore, this class describes all maps coming from simple homotopy equivalences at the level of complexes. The advantage of this theory is that the elementary move of finite spaces is much simpler than the elementary move of simplicial complexes: It consists of removing (or adding) just a single point of the space.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndoi:10.1016/j.aim.2007.11.019 (https://webhomes.maths.ed.ac.uk/~v1ranick/papers/barmak3.pdf)\nciteturn4search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.6 years ago; Jonathan Ariel Barmak, Elias Gabriel Minian * ... We define the concept of *collapse* of a finite space and prove that this new notion corresponds exactly to the concept of a simplicial collapse. ... **Keywords:** Finite spaces; Simplicial complexes; Simple homotopy types; Posets; Weak homotopy equivalences; Simple homotopy equivalences\nAvailable online at www.sciencedirect.com\n\nScienceDirect\n\nAdvances in Mathematics 218 (2008) 87–104\n\nELSEVIER\n\nADVANCES IN Mathematics\n\nwww.elsevier.com/locate/aim\n\n# Simple homotopy types and finite spaces\n\nJonathan Ariel Barmak, Elias Gabriel Minian *\n\n*Departamento de Matemática, FCEyN, Universidad de Buenos Aires, Buenos Aires, Argentina\n\nReceived 29 November 2006; accepted 28 November 2007  \nAvailable online 3 January 2008  \nCommunicated by Mark Hovey\n\n**Abstract**\n\nWe present a new approach to simple homotopy theory of polyhedra using finite topological spaces. We define the concept of *collapse* of a finite space and prove that this new notion corresponds exactly to the concept of a simplicial collapse. More precisely, we show that a collapse \\(X \\searrow Y\\) of finite spaces induces a simplicial collapse \\(K(X) \\searrow K(Y)\\) of their associated simplicial complexes. Moreover, a simplicial collapse \\(K \\searrow L\\) induces a collapse \\(\\mathcal{X}(K) \\searrow \\mathcal{X}(L)\\) of the associated finite spaces. This establishes a one-to-one correspondence between simple homotopy types of finite simplicial complexes and simple equivalence classes of finite spaces. We also prove a similar result for maps: We give a complete characterization of the class of maps between finite spaces which induce simple homotopy equivalences between the associated polyhedra. This class describes all maps coming from simple homotopy equivalences at the level of complexes. The advantage of this theory is that the elementary move of finite spaces is much simpler than the elementary move of simplicial complexes: It consists of removing (or adding) just a single point of the space.  \n© 2007 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\n\nMSC: 57Q10; 55U05; 57N65; 55P15; 06A06\n\n**Keywords:** Finite spaces; Simplicial complexes; Simple homotopy types; Posets; Weak homotopy equivalences; Simple homotopy equivalences\n\n* Corresponding author.  \nE-mail addresses: jbarmak@dm.uba.ar (J.A. Barmak), gminian@dm.uba.ar (E.G. Minian).\n\n0001-8708/$ – see front matter © 2007 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.  \ndoi:10.1016/j.aim.2007.11.019--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSimple homotopy types and finite spaces - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001870807003271)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Simple homotopy types and finite spaces\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Jonathan Ariel Barmak, Elias Gabriel Minian ... We define the concept of collapse of a finite space and prove that this new notion corresponds exactly to the concept of a simplicial collapse.\n\n## Advances in Mathematics\n\nDate: 1 May 2008\n\nPages: 87-104\n\nVolume: Volume 218, Issue 1\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# Simple homotopy types and finite spaces\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Jonathan Ariel Barmak, Elias Gabriel Minian\n\nShow more\n\nView PDF Download full issue\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.aim.2007.11.019\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe present a new approach to simple homotopy theory of polyhedra using finite topological spaces. We define the concept of collapse of a finite space and prove that this new notion corresponds exactly to the concept of a simplicial collapse. More precisely, we show that a collapse of finite spaces induces a simplicial collapse of their associated simplicial complexes. Moreover, a simplicial collapse induces a collapse of the associated finite spaces. This establishes a one-to-one correspondence between simple homotopy types of finite simplicial complexes and simple equivalence classes of finite spaces. We also prove a similar result for maps: We give a complete characterization of the class of maps between finite spaces which induce simple homotopy equivalences between the associated polyhedra. This class describes all maps coming from simple homotopy equivalences at the level of complexes. The advantage of this theory is that the elementary move of finite spaces is much simpler than the elementary move of simplicial complexes: It consists of removing (or adding) just a single point of the space.\n\n## MSC\n\n57Q10\n\n;\n\n55U05\n\n;\n\n57N65\n\n;\n\n55P15\n\n;\n\n06A06\n\n## Keywords\n\nFinite spaces\n\n;\n\nSimplicial complexes\n\n;\n\nSimple homotopy types\n\n;\n\nPosets\n\n;\n\nWeak homotopy equivalences\n\n;\n\nSimple homotopy equivalences--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStrong Homotopy Types, Nerves and Collapses | OpenAlex (https://openalex.org/works/W1995627295)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 15.9 years ago; Crawled: last week; # Strong Homotopy Types, Nerves and Collapses ... Type: article ... Authors: Jonathan Ariel Barmak, Elias Gabriel Minian\n\n# Strong Homotopy Types, Nerves and Collapses\n\nHTML PDF\n\nYear: 2011\n\nType: article\n\nSource: Discrete & Computational Geometry\n\nDOI: 10.1007/s00454-011-9357-5\n\nAuthors: Jonathan Ariel Barmak, Elias Gabriel Minian\n\nInstitution: Un